const intro={"n":28,"title":"Picardía Exposes How Soldiers' Rations Disappear","nums":[1013,1024,1033],"summary":"Picardía says he obtained a somewhat better position assisting an officer nicknamed La Bruja. He describes widespread allegations that suppliers and successive officers take portions of food rations before they reach ordinary soldiers. As the allocations move down the chain of command, almost nothing remains. Picardía also complains about poor clothing, violence and mistreatment of gauchos. He explicitly labels some accusations as camp rumors, so readers must distinguish reported suspicions from the shortages he says he observed.","people":"Picardía as first-person narrator; the assistant officer nicknamed La Bruja; suppliers, commanding officers, a sergeant, a corporal and ordinary soldiers.","themes":"Scarcity and ration distribution; alleged corruption; rumors versus testimony; military hierarchy; structural inequality and marginalization.","words":"raciones (food rations); proveedor (supplier); ayudante (assistant officer); cabo (corporal); sargento (sergeant); puchero (stew).","questions":["How does Picardía obtain a better post at the frontier?","Which accusations does he identify as rumors?","What happens to food as it moves through the hierarchy?","What are the effects on ordinary soldiers?","How does the canto criticize the treatment of gauchos?"],"analysis":"Not every allegation is presented as firsthand knowledge: Picardía introduces part of the story as gossip about La Bruja and the supplier. The diminishing rations are the focus of his narrative satire, and the repeated transfers expose a hierarchy of power. The account should not be converted into an unqualified assertion about every frontier unit.","related":"/en/students/vuelta-canto-27/","relatedTitle":"The preceding canto's critique of unpaid service"};
